    description: "PROBLEM. WI-fuzik (PR #2008) withholds a clean untrusted_input / net_recv verdict while the graph holds request-handler entrypoints (entrypoints.REQUEST_INGRESS_KINDS), naming each handler. It reads ENTRYPOINTS, so a server whose handlers are not detected as entrypoints gets no disclosure. Measured on ~/curriculum_repos/caddy (dev after #2008): 31 non-test `ServeHTTP` methods -- Go `http.Handler` implementations, each receiving `*http.Request` -- and the survey's entrypoint kinds are library_export 65, main_function 1, shell_script 1: zero request-ingress entrypoints. So `unseeded_ingress_handlers` returns [] on caddy.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. On caddy today the untrusted_input database claim is withheld only by unclassified stdlib modules and a catalogue deferred crossing -- the same accident WI-fuzik's step 0 measured for Flask/Express, where declaring the framework module complete turned a path-traversal write into confirmed_with_caveats. NOT measured for Go: whether classifying caddy's modules flips a verdict to a confirming one. Measure that first (WI-fuzik step 0a's method, ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/fuzik_step0_11022026/).\n\nDIRECTION. Detect a method named ServeHTTP whose signature takes (http.ResponseWriter, *http.Request) -- and http.HandlerFunc / mux.HandleFunc registrations -- as a go_handler entrypoint (EntrypointKind.GO_HANDLER exists and is in REQUEST_INGRESS_KINDS), then re-run the caddy before/after. Sweep by mechanism: the same \"handler implements an interface instead of being registered by a decorator\" shape may hold in other languages (Java Servlet.service, Rust tower::Service)."  # 67d0
    fields: {}  # 67d0
